data.content:
## What happened

In C# 14 (.NET 10), new **span overload resolution rules** cause `array.Contains(num)` to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

Expression trees cannot represent span-based method calls, so this throws a `NotSupportedException` at runtime when the expression is compiled/interpreted.

## Fix

Force the call to resolve to the `IEnumerable<T>` overload:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevents the compiler from picking the span overload, keeping the expression tree valid.
